{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Orchestrator close verification — the four remaining ACs proven on live infrastructure and the\ncurrent tree, just now.\n\n## AC1 + AC6 — enumerated INDIVIDUALLY, which is what AC6 demands\n\nAC6 explicitly forbids verifying this by grepping one phrase, so I parsed every\n`aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm` block in `infra/terraform/*.tf`, kept those in namespace\n`rebar/host`, and read each one's `treat_missing_data` and opt-out marker.\n\n**22 alarms** (the ticket says eleven — epic 6202 has since added gate-scratch, docker and\njournal alarms). Result:\n\n    breaching  x21\n    notBreaching + explicit opt-out  x1   (bridge_run_failures)\n    non-breaching WITHOUT an opt-out:  none\n\nThe three forms AC6 names are all corrected in the tree:\n- (a) \"no X published in a period is the healthy steady state\" — **0 occurrences** across\n  `monitoring_1fa8.tf`, `monitoring_s5.tf`, `monitoring_s4b.tf`, `monitoring_88ab.tf`.\n- (b) `monitoring_ws7.tf:57` now reads: *\"The earlier 'the probe fails safe' rationale had it\n  backwards: it failed OPEN.\"* — corrected rather than deleted, which is the better outcome: it\n  records that the reasoning was inverted.\n- (c) `monitoring_foxterrier.tf:76` and `:117` are now `breaching`; the bare unjustified\n  `notBreaching` is gone.\n\n## AC4 — the guard has teeth, proven by defect seeding\n\nNot merely \"the test passes\". I reverted ONE breaching alarm to `notBreaching` in\n`monitoring_9ea3.tf` and re-ran:\n\n    FAILED tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py::test_host_alarms_treat_missing_data_as_breaching\n    AssertionError: rebar/host alarm(s) treat missing data as healthy, so a dea...\n    1 failed, 12 passed\n\nRestored immediately; `git diff` on that file is empty. Clean run is 13/13. So the rule is\nenforced by a test that actually fails when violated, which is AC4's stated verification.\n\n## AC5 — bridge_run_failures: DECIDED, leave `notBreaching`\n\nI nearly got this wrong and want the reasoning on the record.\n\nSeeing `rebar-bridge-run-failures` sitting at **OK** while the Reconcile Bridge was failing\n(outage of 2026-09-05 01:33 UTC, ticket `bairnish-sunbeamed-nandine`), I first read it as this\nticket's own dead-man inversion caught live. It is not. Two things refute that:\n\n1. The alarm requires **two consecutive** hourly failures by design. Exactly one had occurred.\n2. The metric is published on BOTH paths. Measured:\n\n       bridge_run_failures  0.0  11:23 12:23 13:23 14:23 15:23 16:23 PDT   (healthy runs)\n       bridge_run_failures  1.0  18:23 PDT                                 (the failing run)\n\nAn explicit `0` on every healthy run is exactly what makes `notBreaching` safe here: missing data\nis then distinguishable from healthy, because healthy is not silence — healthy is a published\nzero. That is the \"existing documented compensating control\" AC5 offers as the first option, and\nit is now empirically verified rather than asserted. The residual case (the workflow stops running\naltogether, so nothing is published at all) is explicitly owned by the Reconciler Heartbeat Canary,\nper `monitoring_bridge.tf:63-64`.\n\n**Decision: leave `bridge_run_failures` at `notBreaching`.** Flipping it would alarm on every gap\nin a workflow that legitimately does not run continuously, and would buy nothing the published\nzero does not already give.\n\n## Close class\n\n`preexisting` — the inversion was present from when these alarms were authored; nothing regressed."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788575260248178001,"uuid":"569c48fc-51c4-4efb-ba9b-f770d650371e"}